%N A402611 Column of the leftmost maximum entry in the n-th row of A032531.
%C A402611 It appears that this sequence exhibits an overall tendency to increase, although this is not yet proven and it decreases at certain indices. It is very similar to A400354 and shares the vast majority of its terms with the sequence. It is yet unproven that the sequence tends to infinity, stabilizes at a constant, or exhibits any other type of asymptotic behavior. By definition, the sequence is infinite and consists of nonnegative integers satisfying 0 <= a(n) <= n.
%H A402611 Yifeng Zou, <a href="/A402611/b402611.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%e A402611 For n=3, a(3)=1, as row 3 of A032531 consists of 2,3,1,2, of which the maximum value is located in column 1.
%o A402611 (Python)
%o A402611 from collections import Counter
%o A402611 def leftmost_max_index_A032531(n):
%o A402611     counts = Counter()
%o A402611     result = []
%o A402611     for row in range(n + 1):
%o A402611         maximum = -1
%o A402611         leftmost_index = 0
%o A402611         for k in range(row + 1):
%o A402611             value = counts[k]
%o A402611             if value > maximum:
%o A402611                 maximum = value
%o A402611                 leftmost_index = k
%o A402611             counts[value] += 1
%o A402611         result.append(leftmost_index)
%o A402611     return result
%o A402611 print(leftmost_max_index_A032531(86))
%Y A402611 Cf. A400354, A401242, A032531.
